lexer:
	load character -> load state = state.transition[c], if state.emit, then emit [start..current] token with last state or something.


	classes: 14
		space
		newline
		letter
		uppercase or underscore
		slash
		paren-open
		special (//might need a secondary table, or lots of states and classifiers. If we do that, then cache locality gets way worse, as we end up having more and larger LUTs /)
		zero
		xXrR
		bB
		1
		27
		89
		afAF // what about AF? these upper letters count towards keywords as well. So we might need one additional classification here, but it would not really change much.

	specials: (27, including "(" and "/")
		So we would end up with 40 classes, and many more cases if we covered operators and punctuation tokens in the lexer. On the other hand, basically 50% of tokens are specials.

		SOLUTION (???): split up into two classifications that overlap, and use one bit to differentiate them. Then, we can reduce everything to 27 slots. Tokens that are not the same category as the start token get mapped to 0? Or something. Also, we can have a base offset? This will be hard without spilling registers and all that.

		Currently, with ~2 states per special token (2*67), and 18 normal states, and 38 classes, we end up at 12KiB of lookup table data, even if we only use U2 indices/offsets in that table. Which might be ok, but we really don't want to be cache-missing here. However, we could also tokenise each special token as its own token, and then do a post-pass over the token list generated by that, and group special tokens that way. That way, we have a very small working set (12 * 18 or something, so 512B) for the bulk of the file. We can even make it so that block comments do not need branching unless they start nesting.

		The post-pass for the token stream takes the token IDs, parses them, and rewrites the stream, having one read pointer and one write pointer, advancing the write pointer conditionally. At that stage, we can filter out whitespaces, comments, etc., and parse special tokens. This should have another 5-6 cycles per token, with special tokens having that cost per byte. So specials would cost around 12 cycles per byte, while other tokens would cost 6 cycles per byte, and then we also have the other postprocessing steps like keywords and parsing numbers. And then we have another cost of 2-4 cycles per token for the bucketing, although that is better than a branch miss. If we got to around 15 cycles per byte, that would be great. Then, we would have reached the performance niveau of the other threads, roughly. If we could get 12 cycles per byte on average, we could surpass the other threads in speed. And we could definitely get rid of the second tokeniser thread, speeding up the rest of the program as well.

		For keywords, the intrinsics are ruining things a bit because they are not easy to look up branchlessly. Though we can have a fast path for things that are <4 keywords and things that are >4 keywords, for example. That way, most keywords can remain branchless, while intrinsics can end up branchy.

		We can also have a 256-large table for each state, which allows us to output a distinct token type per character in that state. This would actually allow us to probably cut down on some classes as well? maybe not. Mostly only useful for special symbols. Though we could cut down on that table size by half if we insisted on only using 7-bit ASCII. Then we would have either 5KiB or 3KiB. 5KiB of hot memory seems fair. We can then do some dummy reads to ensure everything stays fresh?

		Anyway, it would be quite complicated to implement, and would easily have lots of pitfalls.


