			here we have many sub-states. We might want to do that separately maybe. So we could just consume spans of specials for now and then do a post-parse on them.

	So we could have one global transition table. we start with a starting mask. When we encounter new characters, we check the table based on the new character. For each state, we can say whether we want to leave the state, keep the state, or make a token boundary. That would fit within 64 bits. We fetch the masks unconditionally, and we apply bit operations to proceed state. That way, we should only have bit accumulation operations as serial dependencies between characters. This should allow us to have 2-3 cycles per byte, instead of 5-6+ cycles.

	Finally, we have a post-process step for number parsing, etc. We can also have additional steps for putting tokens into length-based clusters, so that we can have more efficient number parsing for example.
	/)


	State {
		TransitionLUT: State *[TABLE Class];
		TransitionData: U8;
		Bucket: U1;
	}
	parse() VOID {
		state ::= initial_state;
		WHILE(c ::= classify[*readptr++])
		{
			tx_meta: Transition::FLAGS := state->TransitionData >> 2 * c;
			out ::= state->Spans;
			state := state->TransitionLUT[c]; // This is the critical path: 5 cycles to look up the state transition from the current state.

				old_tok_start_p ::= tok_start;
				inc_p ::= tok_start_p + 1;
			tok_start_p := WHEN(tx_meta & :issue) inc_p;
			tok_start := WHEN(tx_meta & :issue) readptr;

			old_tok_start_p->Start := tok_start;
			old_tok_start_p->Bucket := tx_meta >> 60;

			// We may want to lift this out of the loop.
			IF(tx_meta & :needs_logic)
			{
				error();
			}
		}
	}

